{
  "_name": "s32_dual_cr_gateway",
  "_desc": "Dual-CODING-RATE two-layer gateway (2026-07-26, Wave-4 #4). Copy of s09_two_layer_gateway, SINGLE carrier, with each gateway's two layers on different coding rates: layer[0] cr=5 (CR4/5), layer[1] cr=8 (CR4/8). Proves the CR RUNTIME RETUNE: before Wave-4 #4 the sim never overrode IRadio::set_rx_cr (empty no-op default), so getCR() never changed and BOTH layers flew at the scenario's single cr -- the gateway's per-layer CR was silently ignored while metal genuinely retuned. The proof is IN THE STREAM: gateway `tx` events carry `cr`, and equal-length frames show a LONGER airtime_ms during the cr=8 window. Pre-#4 every gateway tx read cr=5. cr convention is RadioLib/MeshCore 5..8 (5=CR4/5). \u2605 SCOPE / CORRECTED 2026-07-27: the two CROSS-LAYER JOINER assertions inherited from s09 are REMOVED, not carved as reds -- and they must NOT be restored: measured, they fail BOTH before and after the CR ruling (no push[msg_recv] carries either payload in either run; the only hits are cmd_reply echoes). \u26a0 MY EARLIER DIAGNOSIS HERE WAS WRONG: I wrote that the window split 'derives from SF weighting and does NOT account for per-layer CR/BW'. FALSE -- node.cpp already weighted by SF, BW and CR (v17). The real mechanism is REDISTRIBUTION OF A FIXED PERIOD, and the owner's 2026-07-27 ruling REMOVED CR from the weighting (SF+BW only; different CR settings are interoperable, so a layer's CR must not steal its peer's window). That moved this scenario's split from 3188/8812 to 4395/7605 ms on gw_4 and re-anchored it. \u2605\u2605 ROOT CAUSE of the joiner failure, found 2026-07-27 and NOT fixed (own design call, BASELINE 26y): the last mile dies at data_rx_timeout because start_pending_rx_expiry (node_mac.cpp:1307) sizes the RECEIVER's DATA wait with active_cr() -- the RECEIVER's own CR. gw_4 transmits on layer 5 at cr8 while l5_* receive at the global cr5, so they arm ~746 ms for a frame needing ~975 ms. \u21d2 mixed-CR links are NOT interoperable in this firmware's RX-window sizing, even though they are at the LoRa PHY. This scenario pins the RETUNE; it deliberately does not assert delivery.",
